---
/**
 * Takko Town case study - a bespoke individual page (not the shared [slug]
 * template). Card metadata (name, tagline, stack, url) still comes from the
 * projects content collection so the homepage stays in sync; the prose and the
 * feature showcase live here so this page can carry its own additions (the
 * at-a-glance panel and the live SVG map).
 *
 * Feature layout: features are grouped into two labelled pillars (the classroom
 * platform, the town record). Within a pillar, majors get the editorial
 * treatment (big media, one per row) and minors follow as compact alternating
 * rows. Four minors are live artifacts rather than screenshots: the
 * stroke-letter system (StrokeWord, drawn on scroll), the language toggle,
 * the sound buttons, and the prefecture map (JapanMap, hoverable).
 */
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '~/components/Icon.astro';
import LogoTile from '~/components/LogoTile.astro';
import JapanMap from '~/components/JapanMap.astro';
import StrokeWord from '~/components/takkotaco/StrokeWord.astro';
import LangToggle from '~/components/takkotaco/LangToggle.astro';
import SoundButton from '~/components/takkotaco/SoundButton.astro';
import gamesShot from '~/assets/images/takkotaco/games.png';
import worksheetShot from '~/assets/images/takkotaco/worksheet-maker.png';
import guideShot from '~/assets/images/takkotaco/town-guide.png';
import feedbackShot from '~/assets/images/takkotaco/feedback-widget.png';
import faceCatchShot from '~/assets/images/takkotaco/face-catch.png';
import wordSetsShot from '~/assets/images/takkotaco/word-sets.png';
import trainShot from '~/assets/images/takkotaco/train-mode.png';
import restaurantsShot from '~/assets/images/takkotaco/restaurants.png';
const takkotacoLogo = '/logos/takkotaco.svg';

const entry = await getEntry('projects', 'takkotaco');
if (!entry) throw new Error('Missing projects/takkotaco content entry');
const d = entry.data;
const meta = [d.role, d.timeframe].filter(Boolean).join('  ·  ');

// Skimmable summary: the five things a visitor reads before deciding to scroll.
const glance: { label: string; value: string; href?: string }[] = [
  {
    label: 'Problem',
    value:
      'My town had almost no real English online, just a page that read like it went through Google Translate once. My classes also needed tools built for slow school Chromebooks and unreliable wifi.',
  },
  { label: 'My role', value: 'Solo designer and developer, 2024 to present.' },
  {
    label: 'Impact',
    value:
      'Around twenty-five classroom games and tools, used by real students, running at no cost on Cloudflare’s free tier.',
  },
];

// The two pillars the feature showcase is grouped under, in render order.
const pillars = [
  { id: 'classroom', label: 'The classroom platform' },
  { id: 'town', label: 'The town record' },
] as const;
type Pillar = (typeof pillars)[number]['id'];

// Major features: editorial, big media. Image is optional: a text-forward major
// is fine where no single screenshot captures it. Body can be several paragraphs.
const majorFeatures: {
  pillar: Pillar;
  title: string;
  body: string | string[];
  image?: ImageMetadata;
  alt?: string;
}[] = [
  {
    pillar: 'classroom',
    title: 'A growing classroom toolkit',
    body: 'Around twenty-five games and classroom tools now, from flashcard and worksheet makers to word races and drawing games, all built to survive slow Chromebooks and a projector. I do not build to a roadmap: a tool gets added when I hit a need in class, or a student asks for one. The range is wide on purpose, a quick vocabulary drill and a whole-class drawing game live side by side, because different lessons need different things.',
    image: gamesShot,
    alt: 'The takkotaco.com games page: a grid of classroom game cards including School Map Game, How Many?, Letter Flip Game, Face Catch, and Odd One Out',
  },
  {
    pillar: 'classroom',
    title: 'One vocabulary database, and how a word gets in',
    body: [
      'Every game and tool that touches vocabulary reads from one typed database. A word is a single entry: the English, the Japanese readings I will accept as correct, and an optional kanji spelling. Two generated manifests map that word to its picture and its voice clip, so a flashcard game, a printable worksheet, the review challenge, and the dictionary all pull from the same source and never drift apart. It runs to 51 categories across 11 groups now.',
      'What I optimized for is how fast a word goes in, because I add them on a whim mid-lesson. A new word starts as one line of text, and that is enough to be useful: every game can already use it, since anything that reads out loud falls back to a browser voice until a real recording exists. Nothing waits on media.',
      'The picture and the voice get layered on after. Images run through a small pipeline: I pull candidates from free photo APIs (Pexels and Pixabay), pick the best in a local review gallery, and it is optimized to a webp with its credit saved. When a photo will not do, I use a Japanese illustration or drop in my own drawing. The voice is batch-generated with ElevenLabs in a clear teacher voice, and the script skips clips that already exist, so adding ten words and re-running only fills the ten gaps. Both are just paths in the manifest, so when I later record a word in my own voice or swap a stock photo for an illustration, it updates everywhere at once.',
      'Grammar sits on top of the same idea, 107 points of it. A point is a template plus typed slot specs, so "I like ___" knows it wants a gerund and not a bare verb, and the sentences it generates come out grammatical instead of merely well-formed. Word classes finer than noun and verb are what make that work, and they are also the part I got wrong first: with only loose types, random fills will happily tell a student they can eat curry in Switzerland.',
    ],
    image: worksheetShot,
    alt: 'The worksheet maker: word categories and page options on the left, a live preview of a printable vocabulary sheet with illustrated drink words on the right',
  },
  {
    pillar: 'town',
    title: 'A record of the town, not a translation of it',
    body: [
      'This did not start with games. It started because the place I live barely existed in English, and the parts that make it specific were not written down anywhere I could point to. The town guide is written in actual English by someone who lives here, covering what the place is genuinely like instead of a machine-translated page nobody maintained.',
      'The piece I care most about is the dialect. Takko-ben is the local way of speaking, and it is fading with the older generation. I have been transcribing it from a local book,「南部田子の言葉」, into a real dictionary: 438 entries so far, each with the dialect spelling, a standard-Japanese reading, and an English gloss, plus a word of the day on the front page. It is slow, and nobody profits from doing it, which is most of why I want it to exist.',
      'From there it widens out. There are bilingual pages for the local shrines, with their history and the deity each one honors, and I want to keep going: more shrines, the seasonal festivals, the small attractions a machine-translated tourism page will never bother with. I am an outsider who got let into this place, and writing it down in a language the rest of the world can read is the most useful thing I can leave behind.',
    ],
    image: guideShot,
    alt: 'The takkotaco.com homepage: a student-painted mural behind "Welcome to Takko Machi", with links to the English games and the town guide',
  },
];

// Minor features: compact alternating rows. Four are live artifacts ported from
// the real site (letters, lang, sound, map); the rest are screenshots.
const minorFeatures: {
  pillar: Pillar;
  title: string;
  body: string;
  media: 'letters' | 'map' | 'lang' | 'sound' | 'shot' | 'none';
  image?: ImageMetadata;
  alt?: string;
  // A screenshot too dense to read in the narrow side column: render it
  // full-width under the text instead.
  wide?: boolean;
}[] = [
  {
    pillar: 'classroom',
    title: 'Games that use the camera and the mic',
    body: 'Two games lean on the Chromebook’s own hardware, in the browser, with nothing to install. Face Catch turns on the webcam and tracks the player’s face, so a student moves their head to catch letters falling across the screen. Letter or Not listens through the microphone instead: a shape flashes up, the class shouts YES or NO, and speech recognition scores it. Both fail softly, so a refused camera permission gets an explanation rather than a broken game.',
    media: 'shot',
    image: faceCatchShot,
    alt: 'Face Catch running: a webcam view with a heart marker tracking the player’s face, a letter U falling from the left, and score, best and lives readouts above',
  },
  {
    pillar: 'classroom',
    title: 'SVG letter system',
    body: 'Every stylized letter across the games is a set of pen strokes stored in handwriting order, so anything that uses them can also draw them the way a hand would: tracing practice, name writing, the ABC games. This is the real system running.',
    media: 'letters',
  },
  {
    pillar: 'classroom',
    title: 'Sounds made from scratch',
    body: 'Game sounds resolve in tiers: a recorded clip if there is one, then a generated voice, and if neither exists the game synthesizes the tone itself with the Web Audio API. That last tier is what these buttons run, no audio files at all. Press one.',
    media: 'sound',
  },
  {
    pillar: 'classroom',
    title: 'Word sets a teacher can build',
    body: 'Teachers sign in with Google and build their own word sets, which then feed any tool or game on the site. A set is just a name and a list of words, so a unit’s vocabulary becomes a worksheet, a flashcard round, and a race without retyping it three times. Sign-in is hand-rolled against Google OAuth rather than a library, with the session in KV.',
    media: 'shot',
    image: wordSetsShot,
    alt: 'The My Word Sets page: two saved sets, "4th grade" and "Unit 4", each showing its word chips and bilingual edit and delete buttons',
  },
  {
    pillar: 'classroom',
    title: 'Feedback to Discord',
    body: 'A widget on the games that posts student messages straight into a Discord channel. I added it as an experiment; it is now the fastest feedback loop I have for what works.',
    media: 'shot',
    image: feedbackShot,
    alt: 'The feedback widget panel: a Japanese postbox illustration above a message box and send button',
  },
  {
    pillar: 'town',
    title: 'Bilingual, all the way down',
    body: 'The whole site is English and Japanese at once. Every string is one component with two slots, so a single switch flips the entire page, and my students can drop into their own language the moment a game loses them. Flip the card: it is the real mechanism.',
    media: 'lang',
  },
  {
    pillar: 'town',
    title: 'Places worth going, written up properly',
    body: 'Nineteen restaurants and the local shrines, each with its own bilingual page: what to order, what the place is actually like, what the shrine is there for. The restaurant list opens on a map of the town with every place pinned and filtered by what kind of food it is. This is the part a tourism site would reduce to a name and a pin.',
    media: 'shot',
    image: restaurantsShot,
    alt: 'The Takko restaurants page: a hanging wooden shop sign reading Restaurants, an English introduction to eating in town, and a map of Takko with fourteen numbered pins colour-coded by category',
  },
  {
    pillar: 'town',
    title: 'Train ticket mode',
    body: 'A toggle in the navbar turns the site into a train station. The link cards become JR tickets with a route, a fare and a serial number, the section headers become LED departure boards, and the town guide nav becomes a platform direction sign. It is pure play, and the kind of unnecessary touch that makes students want to poke around. The whole reskin rides on one data attribute flipping the theme.',
    media: 'shot',
    wide: true,
    image: trainShot,
    alt: 'takkotaco.com in train mode: an LED departure board scrolling tool names, two link cards styled as JR tickets marked 乗車券 TICKET and 特急券 EXPRESS, and the town guide links styled as a yellow and blue station direction sign',
  },
  {
    pillar: 'town',
    title: '"Where are you from?" map',
    body: 'Visitors tap the prefecture they are from and a pin appears, backed by a Worker and D1. It never stores a raw IP: each visitor is a salted hash, so a pin moves rather than piles up. I use it as an icebreaker at the start of class. This one is the actual SVG from the site; hover it.',
    media: 'map',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={d.name} description={d.tagline} type="article">
  <article class="container--prose case">
    <a class="back" href="/#projects">Back to projects</a>

    <header class="case-head">
      <LogoTile src={takkotacoLogo} alt={`${d.name} logo`} size="lg" bare href={d.url} class="case-logo" />
      {meta && <p class="eyebrow">{meta}</p>}
      <h1>{d.name}</h1>
      <p class="tagline">{d.tagline}</p>

      <div class="actions">
        {
          d.url && (
            <a class="visit-link" href={d.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
              <Icon name="external-link" /> Visit site
            </a>
          )
        }
        {
          d.repo && (
            <a class="btn btn--secondary" href={d.repo} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
              <Icon name="github" /> Code
            </a>
          )
        }
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- At a glance -->
    <dl class="glance">
      {
        glance.map((g) => (
          <div class="glance-row">
            <dt>{g.label}</dt>
            <dd>
              {g.href ? (
                <a href={g.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
                  {g.value}
                </a>
              ) : (
                g.value
              )}
            </dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
      <div class="glance-row">
        <dt>Stack</dt>
        <dd class="glance-stack">
          {d.stack.map((s) => <span class="chip">{s}</span>)}
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Overview</h2>
      <p>
        I build things I want to use, and what I wanted was something to teach
        with. So the site took on two jobs at once: a real English guide to the
        town I live in, and a classroom platform my students open every week. It
        runs now as a bilingual town guide, a shelf of games and tools, a
        dictionary keeping the local dialect (Takko-ben) written down, and one
        vocabulary database sitting under all of it.
      </p>

      <h2>Architecture</h2>
      <p>
        Two constraints decided almost everything below. The site has to open on
        a school Chromebook over rural wifi, and it has to be something I can
        keep running forever on a teacher's salary. It is Astro deployed as a
        Cloudflare Worker, and it has never cost me anything.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Astro, static-first.</strong> Most pages ship as plain HTML, so
        there is almost nothing to download and they hold up on bad wifi and old
        hardware. That choice is why the rest of this list exists: with no
        always-on server behind the site, every moving part has to find its own
        way to work.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Durable Objects, stood up but not finished.</strong> There is a
        separate Worker deployed for real-time rooms, where each room is one
        Durable Object holding the state and talking to players over WebSockets.
        Only the ABC race uses it so far and it is rough enough that I do not
        put it in front of a class yet. I built it mostly to learn the shape:
        one object per room is a genuinely different model than a normal
        backend, and I would rather find that out on a classroom game than on
        the larger real-time project I have planned.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Workers for the API.</strong> A Worker handles live weather,
        leaderboards, the "where are you from?" map, and the feedback widget that
        posts straight to Discord. Anything that touches a visitor's IP stores
        only a salted SHA-256 hash of it, never the raw address. Between lessons
        it runs nothing and bills nothing. In exchange, every feature has to fit
        inside a single request: a short execution window, no long-running
        processes, nothing held in memory between calls.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>D1 and KV for storage.</strong> D1 (SQLite at the edge) backs the
        leaderboards and feedback; KV holds sessions. It is a real database on
        the free tier, sitting close to the people using it. D1 is young and does
        not want heavy write traffic, which would matter if this were a product;
        a class of thirty submitting scores is nowhere near that ceiling.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>One central vocabulary database.</strong> Every game and tool
        that touches vocab reads from the same source, so a word I add during
        third period is in the flashcards, the worksheet maker and the dictionary
        by fourth. The bill comes due when the shape of a word changes: one new
        field ripples through everything at once, which is why I argued with
        myself about the structure early and am glad I did.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Running it taught me more than building it.</strong> The site
        started on Cloudflare Pages and is Workers-only now, and two things from
        that move stuck. Build-time variables and runtime secrets live in
        different dashboard panels, and putting a build-time one in the runtime
        panel fails silently: the build passes, the deploy passes, and the
        feature renders its fallback in production while working fine locally.
        Then an audit this August turned up 31 stray files, 1.4 MB of Vite
        dev-toolbar bundles, that had been shipping to production from inside
        <code>public/</code>, invisible to git but not to the build. Retiring the
        old Pages project was its own small saga: it had 157 deployments, and
        Pages will not delete a project with more than a hundred, so they had to
        go through the API one at a time.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Features, grouped into two pillars: majors then minors within each -->
    <section class="features" aria-label="Favorite parts">
      <h2>Favorite parts</h2>

      {
        pillars.map((pillar) => (
          <div class="pillar">
            <h3 class="pillar-head">{pillar.label}</h3>

            <div class="major-list">
              {majorFeatures
                .filter((f) => f.pillar === pillar.id)
                .map((f) => (
                  <article class="major">
                    <div class="major-text">
                      <h4>{f.title}</h4>
                      {(Array.isArray(f.body) ? f.body : [f.body]).map((para) => (
                        <p>{para}</p>
                      ))}
                    </div>
                    {f.image && (
                      <Image class="major-shot" src={f.image} alt={f.alt ?? ''} width={1440} />
                    )}
                  </article>
                ))}
            </div>

            <div class="minor-list">
              {minorFeatures
                .filter((f) => f.pillar === pillar.id)
                .map((f) => (
                  <article
                    class:list={[
                      'minor',
                      { 'minor--live': ['letters', 'map', 'lang', 'sound'].includes(f.media) },
                      { 'minor--text': f.media === 'none' || (f.media === 'shot' && !f.image) },
                      { 'minor--stack': f.wide === true },
                    ]}
                  >
                    <div class="minor-text">
                      <h5>{f.title}</h5>
                      <p>{f.body}</p>
                    </div>
                    {f.media === 'letters' && <StrokeWord word="Takko" />}
                    {f.media === 'lang' && <LangToggle />}
                    {f.media === 'sound' && <SoundButton />}
                    {f.media === 'map' && (
                      <div class="minor-map">
                        <JapanMap />
                      </div>
                    )}
                    {f.media === 'shot' && f.image && (
                      <Image class="minor-shot" src={f.image} alt={f.alt ?? ''} width={f.wide ? 1200 : 520} />
                    )}
                  </article>
                ))}
            </div>
          </div>
        ))
      }

      <p class="minor-more">
        There is more in the drawer, like a train timer tuned to exactly how I
        run class transitions and a name-writing tool built on the letter
        system above. New tools get added when a lesson needs one.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What I took away</h2>
      <p>
        Designing games that are fun and actually usable in a real classroom is
        harder than it sounds. Students are on slow Chromebooks, the wifi is
        unreliable, and you get about three minutes to explain the rules before
        the lesson moves on. Simpler almost always wins, and you find out fast
        what holds up in the room.
      </p>
      <p>
        Feedback is worth building for directly. The Discord widget turned vague
        guesses about what students liked into a steady stream of real reactions,
        and the games got better because of it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The free tier covers everything here. Workers, Durable Objects, D1, KV:
        none of it costs anything at this scale. I came in assuming I would
        eventually need to pay for a real server, and I still have not.
        Serverless genuinely covers a project like this.
      </p>
      <p>
        SVG and SVG animation are more capable than I gave them credit for. I
        used them for game pieces, UI, and things I would have reached for images
        or canvas for before, and they scale perfectly on a classroom projector.
      </p>
      <p>
        Almost everything that makes the games feel finished was free. Google's
        text-to-speech gave them a real voice reading words aloud, and the Web
        Audio API let me generate tones and sound effects from scratch, including
        a few that came out sounding more like Mii voices than I intended.
      </p>
      <p>
        The central vocabulary database was the best structural decision I made.
        Nearly every tool since has been cheap to build because the words were
        already there and already shared.
      </p>
      <p>
        The constraint I did not choose turned out to be the useful one. Building
        for slow computers on slow internet meant I could never let a page get
        heavy, and I still catch myself checking what a new feature costs to
        download before I ask whether it is any good.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .case { padding-block: var(--space-7) var(--space-9); }

  .back {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-5);
  }
  .back:hover { color: var(--color-accent); }
  .back::before { content: '\2190'; margin-right: 0.4em; }

  .case-head { margin-bottom: var(--space-6); }
  :global(.case-logo) { margin-bottom: var(--space-5); }
  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
  }
  .case-head h1 { font-size: var(--text-3xl); margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .tagline { color: var(--color-text-muted); font-size: var(--text-lg); }

  .actions { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: var(--space-4); margin-top: var(--space-5); }
  .visit-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.35em;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    padding-bottom: 1px;
    transition: color var(--transition-base), border-color var(--transition-base);
  }
  .visit-link svg { width: 0.9em; height: 0.9em; flex: none; }
  .visit-link:hover { color: var(--color-accent); border-color: var(--color-accent); }

  /* At a glance */
  .glance {
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 var(--space-8);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .glance-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 8.5rem 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-5);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }
  .glance-row:first-child { border-top: 0; }
  .glance dt {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    padding-top: 0.15em;
  }
  .glance dd { margin: 0; color: var(--color-text); }
  .glance dd a { color: var(--color-accent); }
  .glance-stack { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-2); }
  .chip {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    background: var(--color-surface-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    padding: 0.2rem 0.5rem;
  }

  /* Feature screenshots. <Image> output carries no parent scope, so :global. */
  .major :global(.major-shot),
  .minor :global(.minor-shot) {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    display: block;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);
  }

  /* Features */
  .features { margin: var(--space-8) 0; }
  .features > h2 { font-size: var(--text-2xl); margin-bottom: var(--space-6); }

  /* Pillar: the one grouping primitive both case studies share. */
  .pillar + .pillar { margin-top: var(--space-9); }
  .pillar-head {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-2);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .major-list { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-8); }
  .major-text h4 { font-size: var(--text-lg); margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
  .major-text p { color: var(--color-text-muted); max-width: 64ch; margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }

  .minor-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-6);
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
  }
  .minor {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 12rem;
    gap: var(--space-5);
    align-items: center;
  }
  /* Alternate which side the media sits on, keeping the column proportions:
     without swapping the track sizes too, the text lands in the narrow track. */
  .minor:nth-child(even):not(.minor--stack):not(.minor--text) {
    grid-template-columns: 12rem 1fr;
  }
  .minor:nth-child(even):not(.minor--stack):not(.minor--text) .minor-text { order: 2; }
  .minor-text h5 { font-size: var(--text-base); margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
